<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Claims the domains Nook Plus issues handles under, which is what lets
	     the system offer to save a newly created account password and fill it
	     back in later. Without it the system refuses outright: there is no
	     partial mode, and nothing is saved under any name.

	     Applied to the Release configuration only. Associated Domains is not
	     available to a personal (free) development team at all — Xcode refuses
	     to create a profile with it — so carrying this in Debug would block
	     everyday on-device development for no gain. Release is where the
	     capability can exist, once the project is signed by a team enrolled in
	     the Apple Developer Program.

	     Consequences worth knowing while that is still true: a Debug build gets
	     no save offer and no strong-password suggestion, so the password has to
	     be kept by the user; the setup screen says so, and offers to copy it.

	     Production only. Staging was listed alongside it while this file was being
	     worked out, with `?mode=developer` so the device would fetch the
	     association file straight from the host instead of through Apple's CDN —
	     a mode that needs Settings > Developer > Associated Domains Development
	     switched on, and so means nothing in a shipped build. Both are gone: this
	     file applies to Release alone and a release build has no developer section
	     to point itself at staging with, so the entry was a claim on a domain no
	     shipping build can reach — and a developer-mode association is not
	     something to submit for review.

	     Work that needs staging wants a Debug build, which carries no Associated
	     Domains at all for the reason above. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>webcredentials:nooker.app</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
